stion 6 in Section 7 of Blitvić, Kammoun and Steingrímsson, arXiv:2610.29846v1. A Lean 4 formalization and its recorded verification data accompany the paper.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nNested recursions with ceiling function solutions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/224872215_Nested_recursions_with_ceiling_function_solutions)\\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Finally, in [5] we focused once again on the occurrence of certain ceiling function solutions, this time to nested recursions that naturally generalize the nested recursion (1.1). ... In [2], Golomb illustrated that with appropriate initial conditions Hofstadter's Q-recursion Q(n) = Q(n − Q(n − 1)) + Q(n − Q(n − 2)) could be made to exhibit what he called \\\" quasi-periodic \\\" behavior: more precisely, given initial conditions Q(1) = 3, Q(2) = 2, and Q(3) = 1, the resulting solution has Q(3k + 1) = 3, Q(3k + 2) = 3k + 2 and Q(3k) = 3k − 2.\\nThe basic elements of our approach follow: first, we establish a natural equivalence relation on the set of all recursions of the form s 1 ; a 1 : s 2 ; a 2 . ...\\n\\n... In [2] , Golomb observed that if the one-term nested recursion a n = a n−a n−1 has a solution , then it always eventually becomes periodic; in fact, this holds for any one-term homogeneous nested recursion. This paper, as well as [3], [5], and [4], have exhibited large families of nested recursions with periodic difference sequences. In [2], Golomb illustrated that with appropriate initial conditions Hofstadter's Q-recursion Q(n) = Q(n − Q(n − 1)) + Q(n − Q(n − 2)) could be made to exhibit what he called \\\" quasi-periodic \\\" behavior: more precisely, given initial conditions Q(1) = 3, Q(2) = 2, and Q(3) = 1, the resulting solution has Q(3k + 1) = 3, Q(3k + 2) = 3k + 2 and Q(3k) = 3k − 2. Ruskey [6] has demonstrated similar behaviour involving the Q-recursion and the Fibonacci sequence. ...\\n\\nSums of Ceiling Functions Solve Nested Recursions\\n\\nArticle\\n\\n  * Jul 2011\\n\\n  * Rafal Drabek\\n  * Abraham Isgur\\n  * Vitaly Kuznetsov\\n  * Stephen M. Tanny\\n\\nIt is known that, for given integers s \\\\geq 0 and j > 0, the nested recursion R(n) = R(n - s - R(n - j)) + R(n - 2j - s - R(n - 3j)) has a closed form solution for which a combinatorial interpretation exists in terms of an infinite, labeled tree. For s = 0, we show that this solution sequence has a closed form as the sum of ceiling functions C(n). Further, given appropriate initial conditions, we derive necessary and sufficient conditions on the parameters s1, a1, s2 and a2 so that C(n) solves the nested recursion R(n) = R(n - s1 - R(n - a1)) + R(n- s2 - R(n - a2)).\\n\\nView--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nJL OF DIFFERENCE EQUATIONS & APPLICATIONS\\tvol.18\\tno.6  2012 (https://seoulfric.tistory.com/1544)\\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Nested recursions with ceiling function solutions ... Short Note A note on a fixed point theorem and the Hyers?\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSource file for Index to OEIS (https://oeis.org/locate_tab.html)\\nciteturn2search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;     Conway  , <a NAME=\\\"Conway\\\">sequences related to (start):</a>\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nIndex to OEIS: Section Ho - OeisWiki (https://oeis.org/wiki/Index_to_OEIS%3A_Section_Ho)\\nciteturn2search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;     Hofstadter-Conway $10,000 challenge sequence: A004001*\\n    Hofstadter-type sequences: (1) A002024, A004001, A005185, A005206, A005228, A005374, A005375, A005376, A005378, A005379, A046699, A055748\\n\"",text=True,check=True)
